// Assigning one whole array to another, and text as a value.
//
// Shalimar's arrays carry their own extents and its char[] is text, so
// 'label : "abc"' and 'copy : row' are single statements that mean
// something. C89 has neither: an array is not assignable, and a string
// is a pointer to bytes ending in a NUL that Shalimar's text does not
// have. Both are refused rather than turned into a memcpy that would
// need a length the C side cannot see.
//
// Two refusals in one file on purpose - the counted-equals-shown check
// is only worth much when the count is above one.
fun <> = main() {
  char label[4]
  int row[3]
  int copy[3]

  row[0] : 1
  row[1] : 2
  row[2] : 3

  label : "abc"
  copy : row

  ? row[0] copy[0]
  return
}
